WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.860
Rough seas in the Indian Ocean now threaten to end recovery of the ship from flight 13 before

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:12.060
it reaches port. This is SpaceX Daily, episode 63. Recovery teams have called off towing operations

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:18.220
for the 52 meter upper stage after two weeks of effort in the Indian Ocean. The ship completed

00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:23.620
reentry and splash down intact, yet worsening sea conditions prevented it from reaching port

00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:28.960
for inspection. Elon Musk stated that recovery efforts are not looking good right now.

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:34.740
The outcome leaves open whether any hardware changes tested on this vehicle will appear on

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:40.200
the next flight. This development follows the status reported yesterday on the same upper

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:46.500
stage. While starship recovery faces ocean setbacks, ground hardware at the pads continues

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:53.440
to advance through controlled testing. Mechazilla at LC39A performed load tests using

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:59.340
ballast balls to simulate booster mass. The tests exercised the tower arms and structural

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:04.720
interfaces ahead of future booster catch attempts. No flight hardware was involved in the

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:10.780
sequence. Engineers recorded actuator response times and arm deflection under the applied loads.

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:16.200
The data will feed into models that predict catch performance during actual super heavy

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:21.420
booster returns. Results from the Florida tower will be cross checked against star-based

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:26.840
data before the first catch attempt at the site. With Florida tower testing underway,

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:34.620
Vandenberg continues its steady Falcon cadence. Booster B1063 completed its 27th flight carrying

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:42.500
the NAOS mission from SLC4E. The launch was recorded from the adjacent fire station 45. This

00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:48.040
flight adds to the booster's reuse record under the current Falcon 9 cadence. The booster

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:53.380
has now flown more missions than most others in the active fleet. Range safety and telemetry

00:01:53.380 --> 00:01:59.440
data from the mission remain under review by SpaceX operations teams. A Falcon 9 mission

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:05.020
remains on the schedule for a Saturday lift off from Vandenberg. Whether and range constraints

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:10.460
will determine the final window. Ground teams have completed final vehicle integration checks

00:02:10.460 --> 00:02:17.260
at SLC4E. The mission continues the pattern of rapid pad turnarounds observed at the California

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:24.120
site this year. Falcon hardware continues to deliver data even after its mission's end. NASA

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:29.620
confirmed the Falcon 9 upper stage impacted the lunar surface at the predicted location and

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:35.860
velocity. The event supplies fresh data on crater formation and regolith behavior from a known

00:02:35.860 --> 00:02:42.420
impactor. South Korean orbiter imagery captured the resulting crater for comparison with pre-impact

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:48.700
views. The impact velocity reached several kilometers per second consistent with orbital mechanics

00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:54.700
models. Analysts expect the crater morphology to refine future predictions of uncontrolled

00:02:54.700 --> 00:03:00.940
re-entries. While Falcon stages provide lunar science, SpaceX leadership continues to outline

00:03:00.940 --> 00:03:07.920
longer-term lunar plans. Elon Musk stated that lunar factories remain part of SpaceX's long-term

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:14.760
architecture. The comment ties future in C2 resource utilization to starship landing and refueling

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:20.860
milestones. No timeline or hardware details were provided. The statement aligns with earlier

00:03:20.860 --> 00:03:25.820
public remarks on using starship to deliver industrial equipment to the lunar surface.

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:31.480
Future missions would require demonstrated orbital refueling before sustained factory

00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:37.080
construction could begin. The same compute heavy strategy now shaping long-term lunar

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:44.100
ambitions is also drawing internal capital questions. One thing worth watching is SpaceX's XAI

00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:51.440
pivot now accounts for 82.7% of capital expenditures according to one analysis. The concentration

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:58.100
raises questions about allocation across launch, starlink, and other programs. Observers note the

00:03:58.100 --> 00:04:03.580
shift could affect timelines for hardware reuse and constellation growth if compute spending

00:04:03.580 --> 00:04:10.440
continues at this pace. The report examines supplier contracts and power infrastructure commitments tied

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:16.940
to the expanded compute buildout. Observers on r-slash-space-x-a-lounge noted the official

00:04:16.940 --> 00:04:23.400
tariff-ab promotion video released by SpaceX, with Musk describing the Texas facility as the

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:29.820
largest and most valuable building on Earth. The post included direct links to the SpaceX and

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:34.980
Elon Musk accounts that posted the footage. Viewers highlighted the scale of the planned

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:40.760
structure relative to existing industrial sites. Users discussed the recovery team's ongoing

00:04:40.760 --> 00:04:47.460
efforts to tow the flight 13 ship through rough seas toward port. The thread referenced the 52 meter

00:04:47.460 --> 00:04:53.300
length of the upper stage and the two-week duration of the towing attempt. Several users posted

00:04:53.300 --> 00:04:59.260
updates on sea conditions that ultimately led to the operation being abandoned. Community members

00:04:59.260 --> 00:05:06.700
shared footage of the Falcon 9 B1063 lift-off captured directly from the SLC-4 fire station.

00:05:07.420 --> 00:05:12.500
The video showed the booster's ascent profile from a vantage point immediately adjacent to the

00:05:12.500 --> 00:05:18.520
pad. Viewers noted the clarity of the plume and the timing of stage separation visible in the

00:05:18.520 --> 00:05:25.180
recording. On the AI front, cursor indicated the SpaceX acquisition could close by the end of

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:31.780
with potential GROC branding on future products. The timeline aligns with earlier statements from

00:05:31.780 --> 00:05:38.000
cursor leadership about finalizing terms. Product teams are already mapping branding changes and

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:44.840
integration points for GROC models inside the editor. XAI CapEx is projected to reach top tier

00:05:44.840 --> 00:05:50.740
levels, drawing attention to suppliers for the expanding cluster infrastructure. The analysis

00:05:50.740 --> 00:05:56.300
identifies key vendors for power systems, networking hardware, and cooling equipment required

00:05:56.300 --> 00:06:02.360
for the next phase of colossus scale builds. Spending growth is tied to training and inference

00:06:02.360 --> 00:06:10.000
workloads that support both XAI models and downstream SpaceX applications. SpaceX's $60 billion

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:16.220
cursor acquisition is reported as potentially finalizing next week. The deal would bring the

00:06:16.220 --> 00:06:22.140
AI code editor under the same corporate umbrella as XAI and GROC distribution channels.

00:06:22.920 --> 00:06:28.420
Integration planning includes possible rebranding of certain product lines to emphasize GROC

00:06:28.420 --> 00:06:34.720
capabilities for enterprise users. From an engineering standpoint, Mecha Zilla's recent load tests

00:06:34.720 --> 00:06:41.900
at LC-39A used ballast balls to apply controlled forces to the tower arms and structural interfaces.

00:06:42.620 --> 00:06:48.000
The approach isolates the mechanical behavior of the catch hardware from the variables of a live

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:53.960
booster, allowing engineers to measure deflection, stress distribution, and actuator response

00:06:53.960 --> 00:07:00.360
under known loads. The tower must absorb the kinetic energy of a descending super heavy booster

00:07:00.360 --> 00:07:06.260
while maintaining positional accuracy within centimeters. Any excess compliance would risk

00:07:06.260 --> 00:07:12.460
misalignment during the final meters of descent. By substituting ballast for propellant mass and

00:07:12.460 --> 00:07:18.440
engines, the test removes combustion and guidance noise, revealing the pure structural margin of

00:07:18.440 --> 00:07:23.980
the arms and their mounting points. The data directly informs whether the current pin and latch

00:07:23.980 --> 00:07:30.400
geometry can tolerate the off nominal trajectories expected in early catch attempts. If the measured

00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:36.080
stiffness exceeds predictions, the next design iteration can reduce actuator power or arm

00:07:36.800 --> 00:07:42.740
lowering both cost and cycle time between flights. The ballast ball method also provides a

00:07:42.740 --> 00:07:48.300
repeatable baseline for comparing future tests that introduce dynamic motion or thermal gradients.

00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:53.020
In a real catch, the super heavy booster arrives with residual propellant,

00:07:53.480 --> 00:07:59.440
slosh, and varying thrust vector angles. The static test therefore serves as the zero state

00:07:59.440 --> 00:08:05.360
reference against which those perturbations are later added. Engineers can now calculate the

00:08:05.360 --> 00:08:11.360
idiot index for the catch hardware. The ratio of finished tower cost to the raw steel, hydraulic

00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:17.560
fluid, and actuator components inside it. And identify which subsystems contribute the largest

00:08:17.560 --> 00:08:23.740
multiplier. Reducing that multiplier through iterative load testing is the direct path to cheaper,

00:08:23.980 --> 00:08:30.860
faster reuse of the booster stage. The LC-39A results will be cross-checked against star-based

00:08:30.860 --> 00:08:36.480
tower data to confirm that both sites share the same structural performance envelope before

00:08:36.480 --> 00:08:42.520
the first attempted catch at the Florida pad. Watch for whether future tests add dynamic motion

00:08:42.520 --> 00:08:48.580
or thermal gradients to closer approximate flight conditions. Those additions would move the test

00:08:48.580 --> 00:08:54.660
campaign from pure structural validation toward integrated system verification, the final gate

00:08:54.660 --> 00:09:00.700
before a live super heavy booster attempts the tower catch. The sequence of static, dynamic,

00:09:01.060 --> 00:09:06.340
and then flight tests mirrors the same first principles progression used earlier in the Falcon 9

00:09:06.340 --> 00:09:14.780
landing program. Before we go, the next mechazilla tests at LC-39A may introduce dynamic motion to move

00:09:14.780 --> 00:09:23.600
closer to flight-like conditions. That's your SpaceX news for today. SPC-X is trading at $133.11

